{  
   "requirements_analysis":{  
      "diagnostic_functions_requirement":{  
         "id":"17",  
         "description":"The system must provide diagnostic functions to identify issues with the steering angle sensor and recommend corrective actions.",
         "analysis":"This requirement emphasizes the need for a comprehensive diagnostic capability within the vehicle's electronic systems. It suggests that the system should not only detect problems but also guide users towards resolving them, enhancing user experience and safety by preventing potential steering-related incidents due to sensor malfunctions."
      },
      "fail_safe_mode_requirement":{  
         "id":"9",  
         "description":"In case of wheel steering sensor failure, the system must revert to a fail-safe mode that allows for controlled and safe vehicle operation.",
         "analysis":"This requirement is crucial for maintaining vehicle safety in the event of a critical component failure. It ensures that even if the primary steering control mechanism fails, the vehicle can still be operated safely until repairs are made. This aligns with industry standards for automotive safety systems, prioritizing driver and passenger protection."
      },
      "fault_detection_and_fail_operational_mode_requirement":{  
         "id":"11",  
         "description":"EPS systems must include fault detection mechanisms that alert the driver to steering torque malfunctions and, where possible, provide a fail-operational mode.",
         "analysis":"This requirement highlights the importance of real-time monitoring and response capabilities in EPS (Electric Power Steering) systems. By detecting faults and notifying the driver, it ensures timely awareness of potential issues. The inclusion of a fail-operational mode further enhances safety by allowing continued vehicle operation under compromised conditions, which is essential for maintaining control and preventing accidents."
      },
      "interdependencies_and_considerations":{  
         "analysis":"The requirements are interrelated in their focus on ensuring the reliability and safety of steering systems. Diagnostic functions (Requirement 17) support fault detection mechanisms (Requirement 11) by providing detailed insights into sensor issues, which can be crucial for both alerting drivers and enabling fail-safe or fail-operational modes (Requirements 9 and 11). The integration of these requirements ensures a robust system that not only detects and alerts but also mitigates risks through controlled operation. Considerations include ensuring seamless communication between diagnostic tools and fault detection systems, as well as designing intuitive interfaces for driver alerts."
      }
   }
}

